Leapmotor cloud client (real car data, optional):
  This product includes code ported from LMB10 (https://github.com/txurtxil/LPB10) by txurtxil,
  licensed under the GNU General Public License v3.0 and combined with HeadQLink under section 13
  of both licenses. Only the read-only part of the protocol was ported (login, session refresh,
  request signing, vehicle list and status); none of LMB10's remote-control functions.
    app/src/main/java/com/headqlink/link/LeapCrypto.java  - signing, HKDF key, account PKCS#12 password, device id
    app/src/main/java/com/headqlink/link/LeapApi.java     - login, session, token refresh, vehicle list and status
    app/src/main/java/com/headqlink/link/LeapStatus.java  - signal table, field merging, conversions, charge/tyre rules
  The unofficial Leapmotor API may stop working at any time. HeadQLink is not affiliated with Leapmotor.

Range-extender (REEV) signals and the Leapmotor cloud trip history:
  The fuel signals (3235 tank %, 3263 millilitres, 3259 fuel range, 3261 total range) and the
  per-trip history fields (routeStartTs, routeEndTs, totalMileage, totalEnergy, driveReevOil) follow
  leapmotor-mate (https://github.com/ProtossBlaster/leapmotor-mate) by ProtossBlaster; the weekly
  consumption endpoint follows leapmotor-api (https://github.com/markoceri/leapmotor-api) by markoceri.
  Both are licensed under the GNU Affero General Public License v3.0. No code was copied; HeadQLink
  reimplements the read-only requests in LeapApi.java, LeapStatus.java and CloudHistory.java.
  The only command to the car, sentry mode on/off (opt-in: sent only with the car PIN the user saves),
  follows leapmotor-api's flow (certificate sync, AES-encrypted PIN verify, remote/ctl cmdId 220, result
  query; sentry status signal 3636), reimplemented in LeapApi.remote() and LeapCrypto.operatePassword().

Charging point data (Route tab and charge plan):
  In Spain, chargers come from the official registry of public charging points of the Ministry for the
  Ecological Transition (MITECO), published by the DGT on Spain's National Access Point (nap.dgt.es,
  DATEX II "electrolineras" publication) under a Creative Commons Attribution licence. HeadQLink
  downloads it on the phone at most once a week and keeps a compact copy (DgtChargers.java). Elsewhere,
  and for points missing from the registry, chargers come from OpenStreetMap (© OpenStreetMap
  contributors, ODbL) through the public Overpass servers.

